  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$",
  "worked_solution": "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/e648e3a5f0c844d6596d4848629c3e6749647228116bcc5da6fce250895141d5.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/378016bbd17371e3dfee1e80934e76b1357139d405cb320ac5befa252da82b28.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,-\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png)",
